call_details.response:
The board state has no immediate winning move for o (player to move). Likewise, x has no immediate winning move. However, x has a powerful potential diagonal threat along the line of squares (5,1)–(4,2)–(3,3)–(2,4). The only squares that currently generate an immediate single threat for x are therefore columns 1 and 2.

Column 4 is the unique move that simultaneously
- occupies a key square on x’s secondary diagonal (1,4)–(2,3)–(3,2)–(4,1),
- begins a horizontal three-in-a-row for o on row 1, and
- forces x into a forcing sequence whose own forced replies later enable a winning horizontal four for o on that same row.

Concretely, after o plays column 4 the principal line is:

- x must create a threat with column 2 (otherwise o simply seizes the initiative with column 5);
- o blocks the lower end (column 1);
- x continues the same diagonal by playing column 2 again;
- o blocks (column 2);
- o now plays column 5, creating a threat that x must answer on column 5;
- that forced answer by x makes (1,5) live, so o’s block on (1,5) completes the horizontal three on row 1 whose free end (1,2) is already accessible;
- o therefore obtains a fresh threat that x must again answer.

At every step x possesses only a single threat, so o can always answer; after the final block o is free to begin a second, independent threat (most cleanly the vertical on column 6). Because x never obtains a double threat while o does, the position is a win for o. All other first moves either surrender the strong diagonal to x without compensation or fail to prepare the counter-threat on row 1, allowing x to keep the initiative.

Final Answer: 4

